  "content": {
    "id": "p24-q24-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "24",
    "prompt": "Two lines have gradients $3$, $4$ respectively and $y$-intercepts $1$, $-2$ respectively. Find the coordinates of the point of intersection of the two lines.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(3,10)$",
      "worked": "The equations are $y=3x+1$ and $y=4x-2$.\n\nSubstitute $y=3x+1$ into the second equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}3x+1&=4x-2\\\\1&=x-2\\\\x&=3\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting $x=3$ into the first equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=3\\times3+1\\\\y&=10\\end{align*}$$\nCheck in the second equation: $10=4\\times3-2$.\n\nThe point of intersection is $(3,10)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
